## 5. Required Interface Reachability Contract

This block states the required interface identity, resolution, invocation, input shape, and setup that the implementation must realize. It does not state expected outcomes, prescribe private implementation structure, or expand edit scope.

```text
test_interface = http/service (signals: ['"'"'csrf-form-login'"'"', '"'"'direct-filesystem-access'"'"', '"'"'http-routes'"'"', '"'"'raw-socket-http-client'"'"', '"'"'session-cookie-authentication'"'"', '"'"'sqlite-direct-access'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  A Flask web application from the Mycodo source tree installed at `/home/mycodo`,
  listening on port 80 inside the container. Callers address it as
  `http://localhost:80` (base URL overridable via the environment variable
  `MYCODO_URL`) and make ordinary HTTP GET/POST requests against it.
  The service is started by the container entrypoint, which runs from working
  directory `/home/mycodo/mycodo`:

      python start_flask_ui.py -d -s

  inside a restart loop, so the server is re-launched if the process exits.
  The HTTP routes are registered at the URL root: the blueprint named
  `routes_general` in `mycodo/mycodo_flask/routes_general.py` is registered via
  `register_blueprints(app)` in `mycodo/mycodo_flask/app.py` with no
  `url_prefix`, so its routes keep the exact paths below. The note-attachment
  route in that module is registered as `/note_attachment/<path:filename>`
  (the `<path:...>` converter is staged by the image build), so the filename
  parameter can span multiple URL path segments. The login route lives in the
  `routes_authentication` blueprint, also registered at the URL root.

## Must provide
  GET  `/login_password`        renders the login form, which carries a
                                `csrf_token` field the caller reads back
  POST `/login_password`        accepts the login form; login is CSRF-protected
                                and flask-login session-cookie based
  GET  `/`                      the application root; callers use it as a
                                liveness probe
  GET  `/note_attachment/<path:filename>`
                                login-gated (`@flask_login.login_required`);
                                serves an attachment from the note-attachments
                                directory, which is `/home/mycodo/note_attachments`
                                (`PATH_NOTE_ATTACHMENTS`, derived from
                                `INSTALL_DIRECTORY` in `mycodo/config.py`)
  GET  `/camera/<camera_unique_id>/<img_type>/<filename>`
                                login-gated; looks up the camera by `unique_id`
                                in the application'"'"'s SQLite database and serves
                                an image file for it; for `<img_type>` `still`
                                the image directory is
                                `/home/mycodo/cameras/<camera_unique_id>/still`
                                (`PATH_CAMERAS` likewise derived from
                                `INSTALL_DIRECTORY`)
  `/home/mycodo/databases/mycodo.db`
                                the application SQLite database containing a
                                `camera` table with at least the columns
                                `unique_id`, `name`, `library`, `device`; the
                                camera route resolves `camera_unique_id`
                                against this table
  Session cookie named `session`
                                presented in the `Cookie` header of subsequent
                                requests; the login-gated routes consult it
                                before serving

## Called as
  GET `/login_password`
      No arguments; the caller reads the `csrf_token` field value out of the
      returned HTML form.

  POST `/login_password`
      Form-encoded body with the fields:
        form-name     a string
        csrf_token    a string (the value read from the login form)
        username      a string
        password      a string
      The caller follows redirects and takes the `session` cookie from the
      cookie jar afterwards.

  GET `/note_attachment/<path:filename>`
      filename — URL path segment after `/note_attachment/`; a string. Callers
      pass single-segment filenames, filenames containing spaces, and, over the
      raw transport, request paths written verbatim whose remainder after the
      route prefix may contain further path segments and percent-encoded
      sequences.

  GET `/camera/<camera_unique_id>/still/<filename>`
      camera_unique_id — 1st path parameter; a string
      img_type         — 2nd path parameter; a string (callers use `still`)
      filename         — 3rd path parameter; a string

  Both file-serving routes are also reached by a caller-side raw-socket HTTP
  client that opens a TCP connection to the service port and writes:

      GET {path} HTTP/1.1
      Host: {host}
      Cookie: session={session_cookie}
      Connection: close

  where `path` is emitted byte-for-byte with no client-side rewriting, and
  `session_cookie` is a string. The implementation must therefore be reachable
  by requests arriving over this transport as well as through a standard HTTP
  client library.

## Setup required
  - The Flask service must already be up and answering on port 80 before the
    callers run their first request; the caller-side runner polls `GET /`
    until it gets an HTTP answer.
  - The directories `/home/mycodo/note_attachments`, `/home/mycodo/cameras`,
    and `/home/mycodo/databases` exist (created by the image build). Callers
    create and remove their own files directly inside
    `/home/mycodo/note_attachments` and inside
    `/home/mycodo/cameras/<camera_unique_id>/still/` immediately before and
    after issuing the corresponding request.
  - For the camera route, callers insert the camera row themselves directly
    into the SQLite database at `/home/mycodo/databases/mycodo.db`
    (`camera` table, columns `unique_id`, `name`, `library`, `device`) before
    requesting the image, and remove it afterwards.
  - The login form is CSRF-protected: a POST to `/login_password` only lands
    when it carries the `csrf_token` value taken from the form rendered by the
    preceding GET.
  - Environment variables `MYCODO_URL` (default `http://localhost:80`),
    `MYCODO_USER` (default `admin`), `MYCODO_PASS` (default `admin`) point the
    callers at the service and the credentials; an account with those
    credentials is seeded by the container entrypoint.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner inside the same container drives the service. It performs the
  two-step CSRF login through an HTTP client library, manages the lifecycle of
  its own attachment files, camera files, and camera database rows around each
  request, and additionally reaches the routes through a hand-built raw TCP
  client that writes the HTTP request line and headers itself (preserving the
  path exactly as given) and parses the reply itself. All of that — the runner,
  the login helper, the raw-socket client, and the temporary files and
  database rows — belongs to the calling side, not to the implementation.
```

