## 5. Required Interface Reachability Contract

This block states the required interface identity, resolution, invocation, input shape, and setup that the implementation must realize. It does not state expected outcomes, prescribe private implementation structure, or expand edit scope.

```text
test_interface = socket/service (signals: ['"'"'build-from-source'"'"', '"'"'build-plan-inspection'"'"', '"'"'cli-argv'"'"', '"'"'object-file-inspection'"'"', '"'"'subprocess-launch'"'"', '"'"'tcp-rfb-protocol'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  A CMake C project rooted at /app (project LibVNCServer; the tree is staged by
  the image build). Callers never execute anything prebuilt: at test time a
  setup script removes any earlier tree, reconfigures the /app source into a
  fresh build tree, and builds exactly two executables:

      cmake -S /app -B /tests/_asan_build \
            -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-fsanitize=address -g -fno-omit-frame-pointer" \
            -DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS="-fsanitize=address" \
            -DCMAKE_SHARED_LINKER_FLAGS="-fsanitize=address"
      make -C /tests/_asan_build examples_pnmshow examples_example

  The artifacts every caller then launches:

      /tests/_asan_build/examples/pnmshow
      /tests/_asan_build/examples/example

  Each server is started as a fresh subprocess in its own process group and is
  reached on 127.0.0.1 over a plain TCP socket speaking RFB protocol 3.8 (the
  caller answers the server'"'"'s version banner with `RFB 003.008\n`). The caller
  picks a per-run TCP port (5951-5954 and 5961-5969 in the current runs) and
  passes it on the command line. All paths the caller passes are absolute, so
  the launch does not depend on the working directory.

## Must provide
  CMake targets (project rooted at /app):
    vncserver           library target; its sources include the translation
                        units libvncserver/rre.c, libvncserver/corre.c and
                        libvncserver/hextile.c, whose objects land at
                        <build>/CMakeFiles/vncserver.dir/libvncserver/rre.c.o,
                        corre.c.o and hextile.c.o
    examples_pnmshow    executable target, OUTPUT_NAME pnmshow, runtime output
                        directory <build>/examples, linked against vncserver
    examples_example    executable target, OUTPUT_NAME example, runtime output
                        directory <build>/examples, linked against vncserver

  Build flag plumbing (the caller inspects it):
    - the configure-time C flags must reach the per-file compile command of
      libvncserver/rre.c, libvncserver/corre.c and libvncserver/hextile.c:
      the build plan recorded via `make -n VERBOSE=1` is read, and each compile
      line for those three sources is required to carry `-fsanitize=address`
      and to contain no `-fno-sanitize` option
    - the three object files above must exist after the build and expose
      `__asan_report_load*` symbols to `nm`

  Command-line surface of the executables:
    pnmshow   1st positional argument: a path to a PNM image file, which is
              loaded and served as the whole framebuffer
    example   no positional argument; serves a framebuffer it generates itself
    both      the option `-rfbport` followed by one value, parsed by the
              library'"'"'s argument handling (libvncserver/cargs.c via
              rfbGetScreen), setting the TCP listen port of the RFB service

  RFB server surface on that TCP port:
    - a 12-byte version banner (`RFB xxx.yyy\n`) sent as the first bytes on
      connect
    - a security handshake offering type 1 (None) — callers pass no
      authentication arguments
    - ClientInit accepted in shared mode; a ServerInit carrying width, height,
      pixel format and desktop name
    - SetColourMapEntries (message type 1) for palette pixel formats
    - SetEncodings (message type 2) carrying signed 32-bit encoding ids; the
      callers pass RRE (2), CoRRE (4) and Hextile (5), each run accompanied by
      the pseudo-encodings 0xFFFFFF11 and 0xFFFFFF10
    - FramebufferUpdateRequest (message type 3) handling; callers send
      non-incremental full-screen requests and then read FramebufferUpdate
      (message type 0) rectangles whose payloads the caller decodes for
      encodings 0, 2, 4 and 5

## Called as
  Process launches (argument lists handed to subprocess):

      /tests/_asan_build/examples/pnmshow <image-path> -rfbport <port>
      /tests/_asan_build/examples/example              -rfbport <port>
      /tests/_asan_build/examples/pnmshow /dev/null -rfbport 5969
          (this one launch runs with ASAN_OPTIONS=help=1 in the environment)

    <image-path>   1st positional argument of pnmshow; a string naming a file
                   the caller itself wrote immediately before the launch: a
                   binary PGM (`P5` magic, decimal width and height, maxval
                   255, then one byte per pixel row-major)
    <port>         a string following `-rfbport` (the caller stringifies an
                   integer)

  RFB exchange over the TCP connection to ("127.0.0.1", <port>):
    security type selection      one byte, value 1
    ClientInit                   one byte, value 1 (shared)
    SetEncodings                 struct.pack("!BxH", 2, n) followed by n times
                                 struct.pack("!I", enc); the caller sends the
                                 list [enc, 0xFFFFFF11, 0xFFFFFF10] where enc
                                 is 2, 4 or 5 depending on the run
    FramebufferUpdateRequest     struct.pack("!BBHHHH", 3, incremental, x, y,
                                 w, h); the caller sends incremental as 0,
                                 x and y as 0, and w and h as the width and
                                 height it read from ServerInit (integers)
    One running server process also receives several sequential full RFB
    handshakes, one after another.

## Setup required
  - Toolchain present in the image: cmake, GNU make, a C compiler with
    AddressSanitizer support, and the codec development libraries the CMake
    configuration probes for (zlib, libjpeg, libpng).
  - The build tree at /tests/_asan_build is deleted and re-created before each
    run; the two binaries must exist afterwards. Callers check for each binary
    before launching and fail loudly when one is absent.
  - Readiness is polled by opening and immediately closing bare TCP
    connections to the chosen port for up to 20 seconds; the server must still
    be serving afterwards.
  - The caller sets ASAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:detect_leaks=0 in the child
    environment at every server launch.
  - Ownership: everything under /app is the implementation. The image-time
    build trees under /app/build and /app/build_asan are never launched or
    connected to by the callers, and the container entrypoint keeps an
    image-time `example` instance running on its default port — no caller
    contacts it. Only the freshly rebuilt artifacts listed under Reached at
    are reached.

## Test harness boundary
  A shell setup script (calling side) installs the pytest runner, performs the
  cmake/make rebuild above, records the build plan with `make -n VERBOSE=1`,
  builds the two example targets, and then starts the pytest session; build
  failures do not abort the script — the run then fails on the missing
  binaries. The pytest runner drives everything else: it launches each server
  through subprocess in its own process group, polls the TCP port, writes its
  own PGM input files under /tests before each launch, speaks RFB through an
  embedded minimal RFB 3.8 client built on socket and struct, decodes and
  reconstructs the update payloads in Python, kills the process group at
  teardown, and checks the instrumented build by reading the recorded build
  plan, running nm on the per-translation-unit object files, compiling and
  running a small canary program with gcc -fsanitize=address, and running the
  pnmshow binary once with ASAN_OPTIONS=help=1. None of this — the runner, its
  embedded RFB client, the images it writes, the canary, or the build tree and
  its recorded plan — is part of the implementation.
```

