This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  A Go HTTP server — the SRS demo api-server — listening on TCP port 8085.
  The implementation is a single-file, project-owned Go program staged into
  the image by a source clone:
      /app/trunk/research/api-server/server.go
  (package main; the directory is a self-contained Go module,
  `module api-server`, with Go standard-library imports only). The
  container entrypoint starts it from working directory
  /app/trunk/research/api-server inside a restart loop that re-runs this
  command each time the process exits, recompiling from source on every
  start:
      go run server.go -p 8085 -s /app/static-dir -ffmpeg /usr/bin/ffmpeg
  Callers reach the server over plain HTTP using the Python standard
  library, base URL http://localhost:8085:
      urllib.request.urlopen(urllib.request.Request(
          "http://localhost:8085" + path,
          data=json.dumps(payload).encode("utf-8"),
          headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"},
          method=method), timeout=10)
  Callers also observe the server through the filesystem. They locate the
  running server process in /proc (the process whose argv mentions
  `server.go`, or whose argv[0] basename contains `server`), read the `-s`
  flag value out of its cmdline (resolving a relative value against
  /proc/<pid>/cwd, falling back to /app/static-dir), and then poll for the
  snapshot output directory named after the request'"'"'s `app` field under
  that static directory — directly on disk, and as a second channel through
  the server'"'"'s own static file-server route, GET /<app>/. Callers draw
  fresh names per request, so only a live pipeline can place the directory.

## Must provide
  HTTP listener             TCP port 8085; callers address
                            http://localhost:8085 and the entrypoint passes
                            `-p 8085`
  GET  /api/v1              index route, polled by callers before any other
                            request
  POST /api/v1/snapshots    snapshot hook route (callers also send GET to
                            this route)
  POST /api/v1/clients      client hook route
  POST /api/v1/streams      stream hook route
  POST /api/v1/sessions     session hook route
  POST /api/v1/dvrs         DVR hook route
  GET  /                    static file server rooted at the `-s` directory
  flag -p                   int; HTTP listen port
  flag -s                   string; snapshot output directory, passed as
                            /app/static-dir
  flag -ffmpeg              string; path of the ffmpeg executable the
                            snapshot feature uses, passed as
                            /usr/bin/ffmpeg
  snapshot machinery        dispatched from the /api/v1/snapshots handler
                            on the request'"'"'s `action` field; its
                            per-request output directory under the `-s`
                            directory is what callers observe on disk and
                            through the file-server route
  source/build identity     /app/trunk/research/api-server builds offline
                            with `go run server.go` / `go build server.go`
                            on the image'"'"'s preinstalled Go toolchain

## Called as
  POST bodies are JSON objects sent with Content-Type: application/json.
  Every field below is a JSON string in the object the caller sends.

  POST /api/v1/snapshots
      "action"                JSON string; the handler distinguishes its
                              values — callers send the hook names
                              on_publish and on_unpublish to this route
      "app"                   JSON string
      "stream"                JSON string
      "vhost"                 JSON string
      "client_id", "ip"       JSON strings, present in some callers'"'"'
                              objects
      GET to the same route arrives with no body.

  POST /api/v1/clients
      "action"                JSON string; callers send on_connect
      "client_id", "ip", "vhost", "app", "tcUrl", "pageUrl"
                              JSON strings

  POST /api/v1/streams
      "action"                JSON string; callers send on_publish
      "client_id", "ip", "vhost", "app", "stream", "param"
                              JSON strings

  POST /api/v1/sessions
      "action"                JSON string; callers send on_play
      "client_id", "ip", "vhost", "app", "stream", "param", "pageUrl"
                              JSON strings

  POST /api/v1/dvrs
      "action"                JSON string; callers send on_dvr
      "client_id", "ip", "vhost", "app", "stream", "param", "cwd",
      "file"                  JSON strings

  GET /api/v1                 no input
  GET /<name>/                a single URL path segment constructed by the
                              caller, percent-encoded by the caller

## Setup required
  The server must already be listening: callers poll GET /api/v1 in a retry
  loop (up to about 30 seconds) before issuing any other request, and a
  container healthcheck also polls that route.
  The entrypoint runs the program with working directory
  /app/trunk/research/api-server. `-s` arrives as the absolute path
  /app/static-dir, which the image build creates and opens for writing, and
  the server must be able to create per-request entries beneath it. The
  ffmpeg binary is a preinstalled system dependency passed by path.
  Because the restart loop re-runs `go run server.go` on every start, the
  source file is recompiled on each launch; no prebuilt binary stands
  between the source and the running process.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner staged from bundled wheels into
  /opt/pylibs and run under /usr/bin/python3, whose calling code uses only
  the Python standard library (urllib.request for HTTP; /proc reads and
  os.path checks for the filesystem channel), together with the entrypoint
  restart loop, the readiness polling, and a compose healthcheck that curls
  the index route. None of these belong to the implementation, and the
  engineer does not rebuild them: the implementation is the Go program at
  /app/trunk/research/api-server/server.go. The Go toolchain, the ffmpeg
  binary, and the /app/static-dir directory are preexisting environment,
  not implementation.
