- **Required interface:**
```c
PJ_DEF(pj_status_t) pjmedia_rtcp_fb_parse_nack(
					const void *buf,
					pj_size_t length,
					unsigned *nack_cnt,
					pjmedia_rtcp_fb_nack nack[])
```
  Declared in the retained `pjmedia/include/pjmedia/rtcp_fb.h` (external C
  linkage). Reached by the RTCP feedback dispatch in `rtcp.c` (a separately
  reconstructed translation unit), which calls this for each received RTCP
  feedback packet, and directly by external programs that link the pjmedia
  archive.
- **Observable behavior:** Determines whether a raw RTCP packet is an
  RFC 4585 generic NACK message and, when it is, extracts the feedback
  control information entries. The packet'"'"'s first 8 bytes are read as
  `pjmedia_rtcp_common`; a generic NACK is identified by `pt` == RTCP_RTPFB
  (205) and `count` == 1.
- **Inputs:** `buf`/`length` — the packet bytes and their byte count;
  `*nack_cnt` on input — the capacity of the `nack[]` array.
- **Outputs and errors:** PJ_EINVAL when `buf`, `nack_cnt`, or `nack` is
  null; PJ_ETOOSMALL when `length` < sizeof(pjmedia_rtcp_common) (8);
  PJ_ENOTFOUND unless pt==205 and count==1. Otherwise N is the number of items the
  header introduces: its 16-bit `length` field, converted from network
  order, counts two 32-bit words beyond those items. PJ_ETOOSMALL results
  when `length` < (N+3)*4. On
  PJ_SUCCESS, `*nack_cnt` becomes min(input value, N), and each of the first
  `*nack_cnt` consecutive 4-byte items following the common header is written
  into `nack[]`: `pid` (pj_int32_t) receives the item'"'"'s first 2 bytes
  converted from network order, `blp` (pj_uint16_t) the next 2 bytes.
- **Side effects and interactions:** Writes only `*nack_cnt` and `nack[]`;
  reads no session or global state. In the RTCP receive path, PJ_SUCCESS here
  is what causes the caller to publish a PJMEDIA_EVENT_RX_RTCP_FB event
  carrying `nack[0]`.
- **Required sequencing and state:** Stateless. In the caller'"'"'s dispatch,
  this is attempted before the PLI check on the same packet.
- **Region boundary:** Self-contained: the range spans one complete function
  definition. The retained text immediately above ends with a closed comment
  block, and the retained text immediately below begins a fresh comment, so
  the replacement opens and closes its own definition and leaves nothing
  dangling; `RTCP_RTPFB` and all referenced types are already in scope from
  retained file-scope code and headers.
```

