## Setup required
  - The pjproject 2.11.1 tree must be staged at `/app` and built there with
    the project'"'"'s own build (`./configure && make dep && make`); the seven
    archives in the link line must exist at those exact absolute paths with
    the target-triplet suffix `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`.
  - The image builds the tree with `CFLAGS`/`CXXFLAGS` set to
    `-fsanitize=address -g -O1` and `LDFLAGS=-fsanitize=address`. Callers
    compile their harness with `-fsanitize=address` and link those archives
    into it, and a preflight check inspects the pjmedia archive itself
    (via `nm`) for AddressSanitizer instrumentation references
    (`__asan_report_load...`). The runtime observation callers rely on is
    carried by instrumentation inside the library'"'"'s own object code, so a
    rebuild must reproduce sanitizer-instrumented objects at those paths; an
    uninstrumented rebuild is detected before any call is made.
  - The headers under `/app/pjlib/include`, `/app/pjlib-util/include`,
    `/app/pjmedia/include`, `/app/pjnath/include` must stay usable by gcc with
    the flags above (a harness that cannot compile is a failure).

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner that, for each check, writes a small
  embedded C program to `/tmp`, compiles it with the system gcc and the exact
  link line above, executes the resulting binary through `subprocess` with the
  environment variable `ASAN_OPTIONS` set (leak detection off, halt on error),
  and inspects the process'"'"'s stdout markers and stderr; a preflight step runs
  `nm` over the pjmedia archive, and another preflight compiles a canary
  program containing a deliberate out-of-bounds read of its own to prove the
  sanitizer pipeline is live. The runner, the embedded C programs, the
  sanitizer toolchain and options, and the `nm`/`LD_LIBRARY_PATH` environment
  all belong to the caller — none of it is part of the implementation, and the
  engineer must not rebuild any of it.
```

