  The image build stages that tree there (`WORKDIR /var/www/html`, source cloned
  into `.`, `composer install`, `bin/grav install`), enables `mod_rewrite`, and
  sets `AllowOverride All` so the project'"'"'s `.htaccess` governs routing. Port 80
  is published; callers address it as the base URL, default `http://localhost`.

  A caller reaches the target by writing a page file under the docroot and then
  issuing an HTTP GET against the page'"'"'s route. Concretely, for a page folder
  `99.<route>` the caller writes `/var/www/html/user/pages/99.<route>/default.md`
  and GETs `http://localhost/<route>`. Apache rewrites the request to Grav'"'"'s
  front controller, which resolves the page from `user/pages/` and renders it.

## Must provide
  Page-rendering route         GET /<route> — serves a page resolved from
                               user/pages/<folder>/default.md (folder prefix
                               stripped for the URL, e.g. 99.func-test-static
                               -> /func-test-static)
  Twig processing switch       frontmatter key `process.twig` — when set `true`
                               in a page'"'"'s YAML frontmatter, the page body is
                               rendered through the Twig template engine before
                               delivery (the body is Markdown with embedded
                               Twig tags, not pre-rendered HTML)
  Page frontmatter parsing    the `title` field and the `process` block are
                               read from the `---`-delimited YAML header of
                               default.md
  Cache layers                 cache/twig, cache/compiled, cache/doctrine under
                               the docroot — the caller clears these between
                               page writes so a fresh page is served

## Called as
  GET <BASE_URL>/<route>       route — path segment of the URL (string); the
                               caller passes the folder name minus its numeric
                               prefix, e.g. the folder `99.func-test-static`
                               is fetched as `/func-test-static`
  page body                    the Markdown+Twig content of default.md — a
                               string written verbatim to the file body after
                               the frontmatter; may contain `{{ ... }}` output
                               tags, `{% ... %}` block tags, and Twig
                               expressions the engine is expected to compute
  frontmatter                  a `---`-delimited YAML header; callers set
                               `title: <string>` and, to engage Twig,
                               `process:\n    twig: true`

## Setup required
  BASE_URL                     env var; the base URL the caller GETs, default
                               `http://localhost`
  APP_DIR                      env var; the docroot where pages are written,
                               default `/var/www/html`
  Service running              Apache (`apache2-foreground` via /entrypoint.sh)
                               must be up and answering on port 80 before any
                               request; the caller polls the base URL until it
                               returns a response
  Writable user tree           `/var/www/html/user/pages/` must be writable by
                               the caller so page folders and default.md can be
                               created and removed; `/var/www/html/cache` must
                               be writable so cache subtrees can be deleted

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner that runs inside the same container. It
  stages inputs on the target'"'"'s filesystem — creating a page folder, writing
  default.md, deleting the three cache subtrees with `rm -rf`, then sleeping —
  and reaches the target only over HTTP via the `requests` library (`requests.get`,
  30s timeout) reading `response.status_code` and `response.text`. A bash script
  waits for the base URL to answer before invoking pytest. The page files, the
  cache-clearing rm invocations, the `requests` client, and the pytest/bash
  runner belong to the caller, not to the implementation; the engineer does not
  rebuild them.
