## Setup required
  Working directory must be /app so that the relative path "database.db"
  resolves to /app/database.db; the entrypoint runs `cd /app` before launch.
  The application is started by the entrypoint script, which invokes
  `python trape.py --url <url> --port 8080` and restarts it on non-zero exit.
  Callers depend on the service being ready on port 8080 (HTTP) before issuing
  requests; the Socket.IO polling transport is served on the same port at
  /socket.io/ (Engine.IO v3 / Socket.IO v2 framing, namespace /trape).
  The database module is a module-level singleton shared across stats.py,
  victim.py, and sockets.py; each route handler calls methods on that shared
  Database instance.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner that drives the running service over HTTP
  with `requests` and over the Socket.IO polling transport with hand-built
  Engine.IO v3 payloads (no socket client library). It reads the application'"'"'s
  own /app/database.db back directly with sqlite3 to observe stored state, and
  it issues a benign /register request as a commit barrier before observing.
  These mechanisms — the HTTP client, the raw Socket.IO polling encoder/decoder,
  the direct sqlite3 read-back, and the commit-barrier helper — belong to the
  caller, not to the implementation. The runner is launched via a shell wrapper
  that installs a pytest environment, waits for the service to answer on the
  base URL, then runs pytest against the test directory.
