  init                       CLI subcommand; called with -no-color, also with
                             the -upgrade flag and with -from-module=<path>
  get                        CLI subcommand; called with -no-color
  module "<label>" { source = "<address>" }
                             the HCL block form read from .tf configuration
                             files in the working directory; this is the
                             configuration the module installer operates on
  .terraform/modules/modules.json
                             module manifest path, relative to the working
                             directory; callers read it back as JSON after runs
  .terraform/init-from-module/modules.json
                             intermediate manifest path, relative to the
                             working directory; callers read it back after
                             -from-module runs

## Called as
  terraform init -no-color
      run with cwd = a scratch directory containing a caller-written main.tf
  terraform get -no-color
      same working-directory arrangement as init
  terraform init -no-color -upgrade
      same arrangement; callers invoke it both as a follow-up to an earlier
      init run in the same directory and as the first run in a directory
  terraform init -no-color -from-module=<path>
      <path> — a single argv element joining flag and value with '"'"'='"'"'; an
      absolute path to a caller-created directory that itself contains a
      main.tf with module blocks; run with cwd = a separate empty scratch
      directory

  All input reaches the program through files in the working directory; stdin
  is closed. The caller writes main.tf as HCL text holding one or more blocks:

      module "<label>" {
        source = "<address>"
      }

  <label>   — a quoted HCL string
  <address> — a quoted HCL string; two shapes are used by callers:
      "./<dirname>"            a relative path to a caller-created directory
                               inside the working directory (or nested inside
                               another module directory) that contains its own
                               main.tf
      "git::file://<abs-path>" an absolute-path URL of an on-disk git
                               repository containing a committed main.tf
  A module directory may itself declare further module blocks in its own
  main.tf (nested modules).

## Setup required
  Working directory — configuration and every produced artifact are resolved
      relative to the process cwd; callers create a fresh scratch directory per
      run and never rely on the image default.
  Environment — callers replace the subprocess environment entirely with
      PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin,
      HOME=/root, CHECKPOINT_DISABLE=1, TF_INPUT=0, TF_IN_AUTOMATION=1; the
      binary must operate under exactly this environment with stdin closed.
  git — a pre-existing system executable on the PATH above; it is a runtime
      dependency for the git:: download path and is not part of the
      implementation.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner drives everything on the calling side: it creates the scratch
  directories, writes every main.tf and every local module file, stages the
