## Reached at
  The target is the Apollo web application served by Apache (php:7.4-apache
  image, PHP 7.4) from the document root /var/www/html on port 80; callers
  address http://localhost:80. The endpoints every caller lands on are three
  PHP scripts staged at the document root by the image build:
      /var/www/html/view_file.php
      /var/www/html/download.php
      /var/www/html/delete.php
  Each resolves retained project code relative to the document root:
      require '"'"'lib/init.php'"'"';
      require '"'"'lib/socrates.php'"'"';
      require '"'"'auth.php'"'"';
  Callers reach the endpoints as authenticated GET requests carrying a PHP
  session cookie, e.g.:
      GET /view_file.php?type=ps&num=1&file=<file>
  The session is obtained first through POST /login.php, and callers confirm
  it against GET /index.php before touching the three endpoints. Both caller
  suites travel this same surface: one exercises ordinary operation of the
  three endpoints; the other sends the identical requests while varying the
  `file` parameter'"'"'s value, and treats a redirect to login.php on an
  authenticated session as proof the handler never answered — so it is the
  endpoint handler itself, not the auth guard or any static response, that
  must answer these requests.

## Must provide
  GET /view_file.php   query parameters: type, num, file
  GET /download.php    query parameters: type, num, file
  GET /delete.php      query parameters: type, num, file
  POST /login.php      retained login endpoint; establishes the PHPSESSID
                       session cookie that callers carry on every later
                       request
  GET /index.php       retained page that callers request to confirm the
                       session
  The three endpoint scripts must exist at the document root and keep
  resolving `lib/init.php`, `lib/socrates.php`, `auth.php`, and the retained
  template machinery (TEMPLATES_DIR /var/www/html/templates, PRESTO_TEMPLATE
  /var/apollo/presto_template). The endpoints act on the authenticated
  user'"'"'s submission tree
      <submissions_dir>/<username>/<type><num>/
  where submissions_dir is /var/apollo/submissions per
  /var/www/html/lib/apollo.ini and username is taken from the session.

## Called as
  GET /view_file.php?type=ps&num=1&file=<file>
      type  query parameter; a string naming an assignment kind
      num   query parameter; a string naming an assignment number
      file  query parameter; a string naming the file the request is about
  GET /download.php  — same three query parameters, same shapes
  GET /delete.php    — same three query parameters, same shapes
  POST /login.php
      body: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
      username   form field; a string
      code       form field; a string
      submitted  form field; a string
  GET /index.php — no parameters; the session cookie is the only input

## Setup required
  - Apache must be running and serving /var/www/html on port 80; the
    container entrypoint keeps apache2-foreground up in a restart loop, and
    Apache executes each script with the document root as working directory,
    so the relative `require '"'"'lib/...'"'"'` lines resolve under /var/www/html.
  - /var/www/html/lib/apollo.ini must parse under parse_ini_file(); it fixes
    ACCOUNTS_DIR, SUBMISSIONS_DIR, TEMPLATES_DIR, PRESTO_TEMPLATE and the
    debug flag that governs error rendering.
  - The runtime tree /var/apollo/{accounts,submissions,concerns,dropbox,
    criteria} must exist; the web user (www-data) must be able to read the
    files callers seed there and to remove entries inside the submission
    directories. Callers re-create those files and re-open permissions
    (0o777 directories, 0o666 files) before each test, so the state at
    request time is whatever the caller just staged.
  - The account record /var/apollo/accounts/testuser.hash is staged by the
    image build; callers authenticate as that user before touching any
    endpoint.
  - Unauthenticated requests are redirected to login.php by auth.php; the
    callers hold the session cookie and never follow redirects.
  - Callers address localhost:80 unless APP_HOST / APP_PORT are set; the
    caller-side runner clears proxy environment variables first.

## Test harness boundary
  A Python 3 pytest runner executes in the same container and reaches the app
  over plain HTTP using only the standard library: a minimal cookie-holding
  client that does not follow redirects. Before every test, the runner'"'"'s
  seeding helper re-creates the fixture files under /var/apollo/submissions
  and beside it, with names and contents chosen at run time; after requests
  it checks file presence on the filesystem directly. The runner, the
  seeding helper, the HTTP client, and the shell wrapper that installs and
  launches the runner all belong to the calling side; they are not part of
  the implementation and must not be rebuilt.
